<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Love These Entertainers. – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Famous Panamanians</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> I Love These Entertainers.</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Propose collaboratively the steps to develop a simple project. Interactive: Negotiate the organization of a project or presentation.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can discuss and propose steps to create a project about famous people (e.g., "We can make a poster about Hillary Heron.")</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can negotiate the organization of a simple project (e.g., "Let's write down different tasks for the poster project, and then we can choose the ones we want to do.")</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can describe basic resources for a group project (e.g., "You can use an online biography tool to find more details about Erika Ender's music career." "You can use a dictionary app to find definitions.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 55%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 10%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of famous Panamanians (e.g., Roberto Durán, Erika Ender, Sech, Hillary Heron, Mariano Rivera, Atheyna Bylon). Students work in pairs to identify them and briefly say one thing they know about each person's achievement (e.g., "He is Roberto Durán. He won many boxing awards."). This activates prior knowledge from previous lessons and connects to the theme "I Love These Entertainers.".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models proposing a simple project idea: "We are making a poster about Famous Panamanians. I think we can find pictures of athletes and singers. What do you think?" The teacher models the pronunciation of -ing verbs (e.g., "making," "finding," "training") and past tense -ed endings (e.g., "won," "performed," "inspired") clearly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are we writing a paragraph or talking about a project plan?" (Talking about a project plan.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we need to agree with our partner on the steps?" (Yes, we need to agree.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio dialogue (or written dialogue displayed) between two 7th-grade students, Ana and Luis, discussing and negotiating steps for a school project about a famous Panamanian. The dialogue includes phrases for proposing, agreeing, disagreeing, and using target grammar (present continuous for future, comparatives, past simple for achievements) and vocabulary (entertainer, athlete, achievement, singer, award, win, perform, famous, popular).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example phrases:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ana: "We are working on a project about famous Panamanians. What about Erika Ender? She is a famous singer."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Luis: "Good idea! We can make a poster. What information are we including?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ana: "Let's include her achievements. She won many awards. Maybe we can compare her to other artists too. She is more famous than some other singers."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Luis: "Okay. And for the next step, we are searching for photos online. We can use a dictionary app for new words."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will listen to understand proposals and negotiations about a project, focusing on identifying key phrases for discussing tasks and making decisions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">**Gist Question:** "What are Ana and Luis doing?" (They are planning a project.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">**Information Matching:** Students match phrases from the dialogue to their function (e.g., "What about...?" - proposing; "Good idea!" - agreeing; "Let's..." - suggesting).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">**True/False:** "Ana thinks Erika Ender is a famous athlete." (False) "They are going to search for photos online." (True)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice proposing steps, negotiating, and describing achievements/comparisons using Present Continuous for future, Comparatives/Superlatives, and Past Simple, along with vocabulary (entertainer, athlete, achievement, award, singer, actor, recognition, competition, performance, sports fan; win, achieve, inspire, train, perform, compete; famous, popular, talented, skilled, specialized, more, best).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Sentence Completion & Oral Drills (5 min):</b> The teacher provides sentence starters or cues on the board/slides. Students complete them orally in pairs, focusing on the target grammar and vocabulary.</li>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Roberto Durán _______ (win) many fights." (Student: "Roberto Durán <b>won</b> many fights.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Sech is ______ (popular) than other singers." (Student: "Sech is <b>more popular</b> than other singers.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Hillary Heron _______ (train) for a new competition." (Student: "Hillary Heron <b>is training</b> for a new competition.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Practice pronunciation of -ed and -ing endings.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Proposing and Responding (7 min):</b> Students work in new pairs. The teacher provides project "task cards" (e.g., "Find information about an athlete," "Describe achievements of a singer," "Compare two entertainers"). Students take turns proposing a step for the project using phrases like "We can...", "Let's...", "What about...?" and the partner responds (e.g., "Good idea!", "I agree," "Maybe we should...").</li>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "Let's talk about Mariano Rivera's achievements. He won many awards."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "Good idea! We are researching his best performances. He is the best baseball player."</li>
                    </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction/Peer Check: Mini-Project Planning (7 min):</b> Students receive a list of famous Panamanians (from previous lessons) and a general project goal (e.g., "Create a simple poster about a famous Panamanian"). In pairs, they discuss and propose 3-4 steps for their chosen project. They must negotiate and agree on the steps. Encourage the use of phrases for proposing ("We can start by...", "How about...?", "What if we...?"), agreeing ("That's a great idea!", "I agree."), and disagreeing politely ("I'm not sure about that. Maybe we could...").</li>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "We can make a poster about Erika Ender. First, we are finding pictures of her. Then, we are writing about her biggest achievements. She achieved great recognition. What about adding some comparisons with other singers?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">19 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Group Project Planning & Negotiation.</b> Students work in groups of 3-4. Each group chooses ONE famous Panamanian from a provided list (or one they discussed in previous lessons). Their task is to collaboratively propose and negotiate the steps to develop a simple project (e.g., a poster, a short presentation, a social media post series) about their chosen figure for a "Famous Panamanians Day" at school. They must agree on at least 5 clear steps for their project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher explains the "Famous Panamanians Day" scenario and the goal: to create a project plan for presentation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups choose a famous Panamanian (e.g., Sech, Atheyna Bylon, Mariano Rivera).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students discuss and negotiate the project steps using the target language for proposing ("We can...", "Let's...", "How about...?"), agreeing, and politely disagreeing. They should aim to describe achievements, compare figures, and use present continuous for future actions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example dialogue: "Let's do a project on Atheyna Bylon. She is a talented athlete." "Good idea! First, we are finding pictures of her training and winning awards." "Yes, and then we are writing about her achievements. She won a medal in the Olympic Games, that's her best achievement!" "What about comparing her to other boxers? She is better known than some others." "Okay, and we are also describing how she inspires young sports fans."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups write down their agreed-upon steps in simple sentences.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Teacher Observation Checklist.</b> The teacher uses a checklist to observe each group's interaction, focusing on active participation, negotiation skills, and the use of target language during the project planning discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: Group Spokesperson Presentation.</b> Each group chooses one spokesperson to briefly present their proposed project steps to the class (1-2 minutes per group). They explain who their famous Panamanian is and the steps they negotiated to create their project. (e.g., "We are making a poster about Sech. First, we are collecting information about his songs. He performed in many concerts. Then, we are comparing him to other singers because he is more popular.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of -ing verb endings (e.g., "making," "searching") and -ed in past tense (e.g., "won," "performed").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Appropriately uses words like "entertainer," "athlete," "achievement," "award," "singer," "win," "inspire," "famous," "popular," "best."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Proposals:</b> Steps for the project are clear and understandable.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration and Negotiation:</b> Students actively participate in proposing and negotiating ideas within their group.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Grammar:</b> Uses present continuous for future events, comparatives/superlatives, and past simple for achievements.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word or phrase you used today to propose an idea?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you feel comfortable discussing your ideas in English? Why or why not?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you learn today about working with others to plan a project?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you can improve next time you talk about project ideas in English?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The speaking activities engaged most students, especially the group negotiation. Students effectively used phrases for proposing and agreeing. However, some groups struggled with consistently using comparatives or the present continuous for future plans. I need to provide more structured sentence starters for these specific grammar points in future speaking tasks. The pronunciation of -ed endings still needs more practice. Next lesson, we will use these negotiated project steps as a basis for writing, reinforcing the language learned today.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The project steps students discussed and agreed upon today will be the foundation for Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), where they will write a short report or description of their chosen Panamanian, including their project plan.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>